WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.180
&gt;&gt; So, it's my pleasure to

00:00:07.181 --> 00:00:11.230
introduce Moshe Vardi
today from Rice University.

00:00:11.231 --> 00:00:14.260
I've known Moshe since

00:00:14.261 --> 00:00:17.680
before he was established
as a researcher,

00:00:17.681 --> 00:00:19.990
actually his Ph.D. advisor was

00:00:19.991 --> 00:00:24.240
my first major collaborator
as a researcher,

00:00:24.241 --> 00:00:27.080
who at the time he was
at Hebrew University.

00:00:27.081 --> 00:00:29.625
And he said, "John I got
this really great student.

00:00:29.626 --> 00:00:32.480
And you want to
keep an eye on him,

00:00:32.481 --> 00:00:34.855
this guy's really
going to be good."

00:00:34.856 --> 00:00:41.985
I swear that
his accomplishments and

00:00:41.986 --> 00:00:44.905
awards read like a list of

00:00:44.906 --> 00:00:47.410
a large fraction of
the awards that are open

00:00:47.411 --> 00:00:50.135
to any computer scientist.

00:00:50.136 --> 00:00:54.430
It says three IBM outstanding
innovation awards,

00:00:54.431 --> 00:00:56.120
the SIGACT Godel prize,

00:00:56.121 --> 00:00:58.105
the ACM Kanellakis award,

00:00:58.106 --> 00:01:03.420
the ACM SIGMOD innovations
award, Blaise Pascal medal,

00:01:03.421 --> 00:01:05.660
National Academy of Engineering,

00:01:05.661 --> 00:01:07.495
National Academy of Science,

00:01:07.496 --> 00:01:12.670
American Academy of Arts
and Letters, and so on.

00:01:12.671 --> 00:01:17.335
He also has done

00:01:17.336 --> 00:01:18.720
a huge service to

00:01:18.721 --> 00:01:23.420
the computer science community
by taking CACM,

00:01:23.421 --> 00:01:29.995
which was a really
much ignored journal.

00:01:29.996 --> 00:01:33.160
&gt;&gt; This is being recorded.

00:01:33.161 --> 00:01:36.340
&gt;&gt; And really recasted,
resurrected it,

00:01:36.341 --> 00:01:40.180
really conceptualized it, as in

00:01:40.181 --> 00:01:41.880
a way and a vision
for how it could

00:01:41.881 --> 00:01:44.250
actually be relevant
and widely read.

00:01:44.251 --> 00:01:46.100
And he actually
made it happen and

00:01:46.101 --> 00:01:48.710
served in addition to
everything else he does

00:01:48.711 --> 00:01:50.530
being a famous researcher in

00:01:50.531 --> 00:01:54.620
several different fields and
writing hundreds of papers.

00:01:54.621 --> 00:01:57.910
Somehow he managed
to carve out time for

00:01:57.911 --> 00:02:01.520
10 years to lead that effort
as editor-in-chief.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:04.530
So, I think we all grow old
and regretted that a debt of

00:02:04.531 --> 00:02:08.330
gratitude for having
done that as well.

00:02:08.331 --> 00:02:13.460
Today, he's on a new mission
on the effect that we have

00:02:13.461 --> 00:02:21.775
in automating all kinds of
work and what is that effect.

00:02:21.776 --> 00:02:23.950
First, if we want

00:02:23.951 --> 00:02:28.830
to improve the lot of people
whose jobs are eliminated,

00:02:28.831 --> 00:02:30.155
I think the first
thing to do is to

00:02:30.156 --> 00:02:32.500
understand exactly
what's going on,

00:02:32.501 --> 00:02:34.400
and he's going to be telling us

00:02:34.401 --> 00:02:36.805
about that today. So, Moshe.

00:02:36.806 --> 00:02:43.185
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:02:43.186 --> 00:02:46.470
Thank you for the opportunity
today to address you

00:02:46.471 --> 00:02:49.905
on this topic which is I think
of the highest importance.

00:02:49.906 --> 00:02:52.730
And just to give you some idea
where I'm coming at it.

00:02:52.731 --> 00:02:55.450
So, it's probably
fair to say for

00:02:55.451 --> 00:02:59.500
about 30 years of my career,

00:02:59.501 --> 00:03:01.605
that was my main focus.

00:03:01.606 --> 00:03:04.250
Published papers,
get them cited,

00:03:04.251 --> 00:03:07.045
get a good h number.

00:03:07.046 --> 00:03:11.180
But about five, six years ago,

00:03:11.181 --> 00:03:12.750
I started thinking and I'll

00:03:12.751 --> 00:03:14.640
explain a bit later how
come into it thinking

00:03:14.641 --> 00:03:18.250
about the societal consequence

00:03:18.251 --> 00:03:21.505
of the technology that we are
working so hard to develop,

00:03:21.506 --> 00:03:24.620
and find myself
about two years ago

00:03:24.621 --> 00:03:26.805
in a press conference.

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:30.020
And the reporter from
The Guardian asked,

00:03:30.021 --> 00:03:32.345
"Which jobs will
not be automated?"

00:03:32.346 --> 00:03:35.700
And I said the
conventional wisdom is

00:03:35.701 --> 00:03:38.900
the job that required
a lot of human interaction,

00:03:38.901 --> 00:03:40.265
and emotional intelligence, that

00:03:40.266 --> 00:03:42.225
these jobs will
not be automated.

00:03:42.226 --> 00:03:43.960
And then on the spur
of the moment,

00:03:43.961 --> 00:03:45.690
I said, but I'm not so sure.

00:03:45.691 --> 00:03:48.710
"Would you bet
against sex robots?"

00:03:48.711 --> 00:03:51.130
And of course this
was a soundbite,

00:03:51.131 --> 00:03:54.780
and you see The Guardian
ran with this headline,

00:03:54.781 --> 00:04:00.050
and a thousand newspaper
quoted me on this one.

00:04:00.051 --> 00:04:01.710
So, much so that some of

00:04:01.711 --> 00:04:05.175
my friends now call
me sex robot Moshe.

00:04:05.176 --> 00:04:09.490
So, let's recap the AI story.

00:04:09.491 --> 00:04:13.560
First of all, in a very briefly
just to remind ourselves.

00:04:13.561 --> 00:04:15.470
You know you cannot exactly

00:04:15.471 --> 00:04:16.780
trace exactly where it started.

00:04:16.781 --> 00:04:17.990
You can find some people say it

00:04:17.991 --> 00:04:19.420
start really in
the 19th century.

00:04:19.421 --> 00:04:22.325
You can go back and find
people thinking about this.

00:04:22.326 --> 00:04:24.750
But maybe it's not

00:04:24.751 --> 00:04:27.565
the arbitrary point to
identify with Alan Turing,

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:31.650
who in 1950 wrote
very well cited paper.

00:04:31.651 --> 00:04:33.160
So, we mostly think

00:04:33.161 --> 00:04:35.095
about Alan Turing
and Turing machines,

00:04:35.096 --> 00:04:37.310
but the paper that
he published in 1950,

00:04:37.311 --> 00:04:38.830
Computing Machine
and Intelligence

00:04:38.831 --> 00:04:41.395
published in a
philosophy journal Mind.

00:04:41.396 --> 00:04:44.140
I believe it's
his most cited paper.

00:04:44.141 --> 00:04:46.020
And again, the paper is

00:04:46.021 --> 00:04:47.560
very famous for
the Imitation game,

00:04:47.561 --> 00:04:48.860
the Turing test, but it's

00:04:48.861 --> 00:04:51.390
actually mapping on
the weakest part of the paper.

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:53.395
The main part of the paper is

00:04:53.396 --> 00:04:54.550
a philosophical paper on

00:04:54.551 --> 00:04:56.650
the possibility of
machine intelligence.

00:04:56.651 --> 00:04:59.210
And Turing goes through
a list of objection to

00:04:59.211 --> 00:05:00.820
machine intelligence and he

00:05:00.821 --> 00:05:03.160
decisively argues
against all of them,

00:05:03.161 --> 00:05:05.125
and he concluded by saying "Yes,

00:05:05.126 --> 00:05:08.125
machines will be
thought to be thinking."

00:05:08.126 --> 00:05:10.525
He said, "By
the end of the century,

00:05:10.526 --> 00:05:14.525
we will talk as if
machines are thinking."

00:05:14.526 --> 00:05:18.110
Well, the century
has come and gone,

00:05:18.111 --> 00:05:20.550
and I don't think still
I will say, "Well,

00:05:20.551 --> 00:05:21.770
my laptop is thinking about

00:05:21.771 --> 00:05:23.160
what's the next slide to show."

00:05:23.161 --> 00:05:25.040
I don't think that's
how we talk about.

00:05:25.041 --> 00:05:29.360
And indeed, overoptimism was

00:05:29.361 --> 00:05:31.955
a common feature
in the AI field.

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:35.480
You go back, Simon
and Newell,1958,

00:05:35.481 --> 00:05:38.750
10 years marks we
will solve chess.

00:05:38.751 --> 00:05:44.265
AI will take us maybe
30 years, said Minsky, 1967.

00:05:44.266 --> 00:05:47.025
So because of
this early optimism,

00:05:47.026 --> 00:05:49.720
the old periods became
known as AI Winters

00:05:49.721 --> 00:05:52.320
where the field have almost
fallen into disrepute.

00:05:52.321 --> 00:05:53.875
When I was a Ph.D student,

00:05:53.876 --> 00:05:56.120
AI was a suspicious field.

00:05:56.121 --> 00:05:59.445
People who overpromise
and underdeliver,

00:05:59.446 --> 00:06:01.160
you can always come up with

00:06:01.161 --> 00:06:03.880
some names that are
associated with these era.

00:06:03.881 --> 00:06:09.995
And to me the turning point
on a personal level, on 1997.

00:06:09.996 --> 00:06:12.310
So, I left IBM.

00:06:12.311 --> 00:06:14.070
I was already at
Rice University.

00:06:14.071 --> 00:06:16.350
I had a very good
connection with IBM.

00:06:16.351 --> 00:06:18.495
IBM invited me to

00:06:18.496 --> 00:06:21.915
come and watch the game
between Kasparov and Deep Blue.

00:06:21.916 --> 00:06:23.390
They pay my flight, they pay for

00:06:23.391 --> 00:06:25.425
the hotel, I couldn't say no.

00:06:25.426 --> 00:06:29.055
So, I go there and I
watched the first game.

00:06:29.056 --> 00:06:33.335
In the first game,
Kasparov was white,

00:06:33.336 --> 00:06:36.030
which is perceived
to have an advantage,

00:06:36.031 --> 00:06:38.745
and Kasparov won the first game.

00:06:38.746 --> 00:06:40.340
And I remember
that then thinking,

00:06:40.341 --> 00:06:42.835
"Okay I believe that
one day computer will win,

00:06:42.836 --> 00:06:44.990
but probably the time
has not come."

00:06:44.991 --> 00:06:47.120
And we're still
fairly new in Houston,

00:06:47.121 --> 00:06:49.100
we didn't have many friends,
I let my wife behind,

00:06:49.101 --> 00:06:51.470
I said okay, I'm going
to cut the visit short

00:06:51.471 --> 00:06:52.855
and I'm going to go back home,

00:06:52.856 --> 00:06:54.760
and I miss the second game.

00:06:54.761 --> 00:06:58.310
The second game,
Deep Blue was white.

00:06:58.311 --> 00:07:01.915
Kasparov had to play
more aggressive game,

00:07:01.916 --> 00:07:03.985
and he lay the trap
to Deep Blue,

00:07:03.986 --> 00:07:06.785
and Deep Blue not only did
not fall into the trap,

00:07:06.786 --> 00:07:08.510
but came out of it with

00:07:08.511 --> 00:07:10.785
a sequence of moves
that were so brilliant.

00:07:10.786 --> 00:07:13.860
If you see Kasparov's
reaction to that,

00:07:13.861 --> 00:07:16.930
he walks off, he
conceded the game,

00:07:16.931 --> 00:07:18.350
he walks off the game.

00:07:18.351 --> 00:07:19.850
And for years, he claimed

00:07:19.851 --> 00:07:21.360
that it was not
really Deep Blue.

00:07:21.361 --> 00:07:22.970
There must have
been a secretive of

00:07:22.971 --> 00:07:26.700
Grandmaster playing against him.

00:07:26.701 --> 00:07:28.720
Just last year, he
published finally

00:07:28.721 --> 00:07:31.500
a book to the 20th
anniversary of this.

00:07:31.501 --> 00:07:32.715
So, he said finally yes,

00:07:32.716 --> 00:07:36.085
it was the computer has
won, he conceded it.

00:07:36.086 --> 00:07:38.460
But that was really
a turning point

00:07:38.461 --> 00:07:41.590
because he promised that for
years there were sometime,

00:07:41.591 --> 00:07:42.640
the future, suddenly it was

00:07:42.641 --> 00:07:46.660
the first significant
accomplishment by AI.

00:07:46.661 --> 00:07:50.930
And then in 2011,
IBM did it again.

00:07:50.931 --> 00:07:52.590
This time with Watson,

00:07:52.591 --> 00:07:59.520
and now it was in Jeopardy.

00:07:59.521 --> 00:08:03.310
Chess is a computational game.

00:08:03.311 --> 00:08:06.700
If you can just look deeply
enough into the game tree,

00:08:06.701 --> 00:08:09.135
you can defeat humans.

00:08:09.136 --> 00:08:10.630
But Jeopardy require whole

00:08:10.631 --> 00:08:12.050
different type of intelligence.

00:08:12.051 --> 00:08:15.230
You have to answer
clever questions

00:08:15.231 --> 00:08:18.920
about airports in Chicago

00:08:18.921 --> 00:08:21.085
that had something to
do with World War II.

00:08:21.086 --> 00:08:23.810
And you have to think,
"Well, it's midway airport."

00:08:23.811 --> 00:08:25.920
And maybe to require
some kind of knowledge

00:08:25.921 --> 00:08:29.430
of culture and history,
and language skills.

00:08:29.431 --> 00:08:32.820
So, that was the moment
that for me I said, "Wow.

00:08:32.821 --> 00:08:35.260
It's not just about
some remote future,

00:08:35.261 --> 00:08:37.625
this is actually happening."

00:08:37.626 --> 00:08:41.470
Now, I think the next
significant accomplishment

00:08:41.471 --> 00:08:43.880
I would put what
happened last year,

00:08:43.881 --> 00:08:48.800
which is now the mantle
has moved to IBM to Google,

00:08:48.801 --> 00:08:52.395
and AlphaGo beat
Lee Se-dol in Go.

00:08:52.396 --> 00:08:54.640
Now Go, you can see here,

00:08:54.641 --> 00:08:56.590
Go in some sense,
a simpler game.

00:08:56.591 --> 00:08:58.795
You'll have a 19 by 19 board,

00:08:58.796 --> 00:09:01.045
just black and white pieces.

00:09:01.046 --> 00:09:03.615
There are many, many
more configurations.

00:09:03.616 --> 00:09:08.120
So the game tree is known to
be much, much, much bigger.

00:09:08.121 --> 00:09:11.380
And therefore, Go
has been considered

00:09:11.381 --> 00:09:12.540
a challenge after chess.

00:09:12.541 --> 00:09:15.545
People have tried many,
many years to do better in Go,

00:09:15.546 --> 00:09:19.550
and deterministic self
clear was not going to work,

00:09:19.551 --> 00:09:21.385
so AlphaGo use, first of all,

00:09:21.386 --> 00:09:23.285
randomize tree stars technique.

00:09:23.286 --> 00:09:24.805
But still that's is not enough,

00:09:24.806 --> 00:09:26.580
so now they use
machine learning.

00:09:26.581 --> 00:09:28.850
And would have
done first of all,

00:09:28.851 --> 00:09:30.885
they digested
all published games,

00:09:30.886 --> 00:09:34.660
and then AlphaGo stop
playing against itself and

00:09:34.661 --> 00:09:38.455
built a big deep model
of how to make,

00:09:38.456 --> 00:09:41.360
what are good configuration,
what are good moves.

00:09:41.361 --> 00:09:44.010
This is philosophically
significance

00:09:44.011 --> 00:09:45.390
because it means now that

00:09:45.391 --> 00:09:49.340
the way AlphaGo played is
by having deep intuition.

00:09:49.341 --> 00:09:51.450
Not having good rules
how to play,

00:09:51.451 --> 00:09:53.415
but having good intuition.

00:09:53.416 --> 00:09:55.830
And to understand
the significance of these,

00:09:55.831 --> 00:09:58.440
we have to go back
more than 50 years,

00:09:58.441 --> 00:10:00.805
and find the
philosophy of Polanyi.

00:10:00.806 --> 00:10:03.235
Karl Polanyi who wrote in 1966,

00:10:03.236 --> 00:10:05.745
and this become known
as Polanyi's Paradox.

00:10:05.746 --> 00:10:09.030
How can we ever hope to
have AI when there are many,

00:10:09.031 --> 00:10:10.730
many things that we can do,

00:10:10.731 --> 00:10:13.010
but we cannot explain
how we do that?

00:10:13.011 --> 00:10:17.285
If you think about how
you drive, you just drive.

00:10:17.286 --> 00:10:19.650
You don't have a set of
rules that you follow.

00:10:19.651 --> 00:10:21.060
And the thought was,
if you don't have

00:10:21.061 --> 00:10:22.665
a set of rules that we follow,

00:10:22.666 --> 00:10:24.545
it's all about
intuitive feeling,

00:10:24.546 --> 00:10:26.430
how can we ever program this?

00:10:26.431 --> 00:10:27.930
And the answer is,
then we know what

00:10:27.931 --> 00:10:29.385
is the answer, right?
Machine Learning.

00:10:29.386 --> 00:10:31.570
We're going to learn
them from data.

00:10:31.571 --> 00:10:35.240
And it's interesting
that the Polliani put

00:10:35.241 --> 00:10:38.305
driving as the kind
of a challenge for AI,

00:10:38.306 --> 00:10:40.120
because it has been
a grand challenge for

00:10:40.121 --> 00:10:42.125
AI now for actually
more than a decade.

00:10:42.126 --> 00:10:44.275
Going back in 2005,

00:10:44.276 --> 00:10:46.090
the DARPA Grand Challenge,

00:10:46.091 --> 00:10:49.150
eventually won by Stanford Team.

00:10:49.151 --> 00:10:50.560
This is Sebastian Thrun,

00:10:50.561 --> 00:10:53.150
in more than 130 miles

00:10:53.151 --> 00:10:56.040
in unrehearsed trail
in Death Valley.

00:10:56.041 --> 00:11:01.295
And two years later, a CMU
now doing it an urban area.

00:11:01.296 --> 00:11:04.670
And notice, this is
more than 10 years ago.

00:11:04.671 --> 00:11:07.450
This is before we had
all the cheap sensor that we

00:11:07.451 --> 00:11:10.840
have today and before we
had the deep learning,

00:11:10.841 --> 00:11:12.300
already people were making

00:11:12.301 --> 00:11:16.665
significant progress
in automated diving.

00:11:16.666 --> 00:11:19.015
And of course, by
now, this picture is,

00:11:19.016 --> 00:11:21.030
I think everybody
here recognizes

00:11:21.031 --> 00:11:23.150
the so-called the Google Car

00:11:23.151 --> 00:11:24.915
or more formally the Waymo car.

00:11:24.916 --> 00:11:31.150
Right? And so, I think
this is an amazing thing,

00:11:31.151 --> 00:11:32.700
it was going to
happen. I'll talk more.

00:11:32.701 --> 00:11:33.710
I want to talk about it, because

00:11:33.711 --> 00:11:35.160
I think this is going to be

00:11:35.161 --> 00:11:38.940
the most significant
technical development that

00:11:38.941 --> 00:11:40.760
I'm going to see in
my lifetime as I expect this

00:11:40.761 --> 00:11:42.910
too for many of you, okay?

00:11:42.911 --> 00:11:44.170
Unless you're very young,

00:11:44.171 --> 00:11:45.335
and then maybe you'll
see other thing.

00:11:45.336 --> 00:11:47.690
But for most of us,
I think this is it.

00:11:47.691 --> 00:11:50.165
And let me put it in a context,

00:11:50.166 --> 00:11:52.005
why I think this
is so significant?

00:11:52.006 --> 00:11:55.065
It's going to launch another
transportation revolution.

00:11:55.066 --> 00:11:56.640
What's the first one?

00:11:56.641 --> 00:12:00.260
Invention of the wheel,
about 5,000 years BC.

00:12:00.261 --> 00:12:01.905
What has he done to us?

00:12:01.906 --> 00:12:03.870
Well, we'll not been able to

00:12:03.871 --> 00:12:06.140
build the pyramids if we
didn't have wheels, right?

00:12:06.141 --> 00:12:09.870
You can't carry this big
boulders on your back.

00:12:09.871 --> 00:12:12.065
What's the next one after that?

00:12:12.066 --> 00:12:14.180
The domestication of the horse.

00:12:14.181 --> 00:12:16.060
Okay. Until then, if you want

00:12:16.061 --> 00:12:17.870
to go somewhere you
had to walk there.

00:12:17.871 --> 00:12:19.470
Now, you can ride
there, you don't

00:12:19.471 --> 00:12:20.990
have to work on your own feet.

00:12:20.991 --> 00:12:23.285
And, if you think about it,

00:12:23.286 --> 00:12:24.725
you have the Mongol Empire,

00:12:24.726 --> 00:12:27.285
which is the biggest
land empire ever,

00:12:27.286 --> 00:12:31.210
all concurred on a horseback.

00:12:31.211 --> 00:12:34.280
After the domestication
of the horse,

00:12:34.281 --> 00:12:38.075
which happen more
than 5,000 years ago.

00:12:38.076 --> 00:12:42.075
Nothing significant happens
in land transportation,

00:12:42.076 --> 00:12:45.425
I'm ignoring sea,
water transportation,

00:12:45.426 --> 00:12:48.000
