00:24:52.346 --> 00:24:54.445
the labor force in

00:24:54.446 --> 00:24:58.295
manufacturing and now we
are at about 8 percent.

00:24:58.296 --> 00:25:00.405
So why is it happening?

00:25:00.406 --> 00:25:02.965
Well, it's very clear
why it is happening.

00:25:02.966 --> 00:25:04.605
It is happening because

00:25:04.606 --> 00:25:06.830
manufacturing productivity
has increased.

00:25:06.831 --> 00:25:10.135
Over the past 20 years,
it has roughly doubled.

00:25:10.136 --> 00:25:11.795
In effect, it's surprising.

00:25:11.796 --> 00:25:13.915
We only lost one third
of the labor force in

00:25:13.916 --> 00:25:15.155
manufacturing other than half

00:25:15.156 --> 00:25:17.600
of the labor force
in manufacturing.

00:25:17.601 --> 00:25:20.865
&gt;&gt; Don't you ignore
the China effect?

00:25:20.866 --> 00:25:27.590
&gt;&gt; So, the point
is if volume is up,

00:25:27.591 --> 00:25:29.920
now what is the role
of China here?

00:25:29.921 --> 00:25:32.240
So because of
the globalized world,

00:25:32.241 --> 00:25:36.440
the manufacturing worldwide
is more competitive.

00:25:36.441 --> 00:25:38.275
And what it meant that US had to

00:25:38.276 --> 00:25:41.815
shift from low
margin industries.

00:25:41.816 --> 00:25:44.515
So we don't make sneaker
anymore in the United States.

00:25:44.516 --> 00:25:47.380
This shirt was probably not
made in the United States.

00:25:47.381 --> 00:25:50.685
We moved the manufacturing
to high margin,

00:25:50.686 --> 00:25:53.710
high capital
intensive industries.

00:25:53.711 --> 00:25:55.735
So when I said that
interest is going up,

00:25:55.736 --> 00:25:57.315
it does not mean
that all is that -

00:25:57.316 --> 00:25:59.490
there's no huge change
underneath.

00:25:59.491 --> 00:26:02.255
And automation globalization
are tied together.

00:26:02.256 --> 00:26:04.955
And automation of
globalization is done,

00:26:04.956 --> 00:26:08.510
it increase
the competitive level,

00:26:08.511 --> 00:26:12.195
and that enables automation
to become more profitable.

00:26:12.196 --> 00:26:14.415
Automation would
have happened anyway,

00:26:14.416 --> 00:26:16.830
because even in this country,
it's very competitive.

00:26:16.831 --> 00:26:18.955
So even if we don't have
to compete with China,

00:26:18.956 --> 00:26:20.385
one company will automate,

00:26:20.386 --> 00:26:22.715
it force the other
company to automate.

00:26:22.716 --> 00:26:25.160
And if you want
to see automation,

00:26:25.161 --> 00:26:28.135
just Google, excuse
me, search, search.

00:26:28.136 --> 00:26:31.055
Tesla Model S factory Floor,

00:26:31.056 --> 00:26:33.120
and you can get very nice videos

00:26:33.121 --> 00:26:35.355
of the Tesla factory floor,

00:26:35.356 --> 00:26:37.615
where they will tell you
the only reason the lights

00:26:37.616 --> 00:26:40.330
are on so they can
take the picture.

00:26:40.331 --> 00:26:43.400
Otherwise it could be
a dark factory floor.

00:26:43.401 --> 00:26:45.785
And it reminds me of what
used to be an old joke in

00:26:45.786 --> 00:26:48.340
GM when they started
putting industrial robots.

00:26:48.341 --> 00:26:50.385
The joke was that the factory of

00:26:50.386 --> 00:26:53.325
the future will have all robots,

00:26:53.326 --> 00:26:56.865
one man, and one dog.

00:26:56.866 --> 00:26:59.005
And the purpose of the dog is

00:26:59.006 --> 00:27:01.725
to make sure that the man
does not touch the machines.

00:27:01.726 --> 00:27:07.345
And the role of the men
is to feed the dog.

00:27:07.705 --> 00:27:11.575
So now, we can ask,

00:27:11.576 --> 00:27:12.775
"Okay, all these happen.

00:27:12.776 --> 00:27:13.915
All these millions
of people lost

00:27:13.916 --> 00:27:16.105
their job, what happened?"

00:27:16.106 --> 00:27:17.955
This is a good task as
what happened, right?

00:27:17.956 --> 00:27:20.835
So the neoliberal economy
will tell you,

00:27:20.836 --> 00:27:22.470
"Oh well, they should
find other jobs.

00:27:22.471 --> 00:27:24.230
There should be a
new equilibrium."

00:27:24.231 --> 00:27:27.460
So now, let's go and examine
what really happened.

00:27:27.461 --> 00:27:29.975
And in a nutshell what I want to

00:27:29.976 --> 00:27:31.945
show you is the last
40 years have been

00:27:31.946 --> 00:27:33.975
actually very harsh on

00:27:33.976 --> 00:27:35.675
working class and middle class

00:27:35.676 --> 00:27:37.775
low middle class, the Americans.

00:27:37.776 --> 00:27:39.705
And this is something
that I have to say.

00:27:39.706 --> 00:27:42.980
The data that I shared
came as a surprise to me.

00:27:42.981 --> 00:27:45.215
And I've been reflecting
on it why is that?

00:27:45.216 --> 00:27:48.405
And I've concluded that
we hear a lot about

00:27:48.406 --> 00:27:50.875
the one percent but

00:27:50.876 --> 00:27:52.305
the real divide
in society is not

00:27:52.306 --> 00:27:54.190
the one percent, the 99 percent.

00:27:54.191 --> 00:27:57.040
It's the 20 percent
versus the 80 percent.

00:27:57.041 --> 00:27:58.450
And we're in 20 percent,

00:27:58.451 --> 00:28:00.400
all of you are part
of the 20 percent

00:28:00.401 --> 00:28:02.785
because you are
educated professionals.

00:28:02.786 --> 00:28:04.575
And that's roughly people with

00:28:04.576 --> 00:28:07.265
undergraduate, some
post-graduate education.

00:28:07.266 --> 00:28:09.425
That's about 20 percent
of the population.

00:28:09.426 --> 00:28:11.215
And for these people, if you

00:28:11.216 --> 00:28:13.315
think about how you
spend your life,

00:28:13.316 --> 00:28:15.425
I would bet that
the vast majority of

00:28:15.426 --> 00:28:18.145
your friends belong
to the top 20 percent,

00:28:18.146 --> 00:28:20.795
and if you have a spouse,

00:28:20.796 --> 00:28:23.270
most likely your spouse
come from the same group.

00:28:23.271 --> 00:28:25.925
So this group creates
its own bubble.

00:28:25.926 --> 00:28:28.070
We all live inside this bubble,

00:28:28.071 --> 00:28:30.175
and everything is filtered
through that bubble and

00:28:30.176 --> 00:28:32.250
we see very little what's
happening outside that bubble.

00:28:32.251 --> 00:28:34.945
We have little idea
what's happening

00:28:34.946 --> 00:28:38.635
in decaying towns in
the South or in the Midwest.

00:28:38.636 --> 00:28:41.555
And the bottom line
is that I want to show

00:28:41.556 --> 00:28:43.945
how the automation poses a very,

00:28:43.946 --> 00:28:45.815
very serious challenge
to developed economies.

00:28:45.816 --> 00:28:48.375
We can discuss
developing economies,

00:28:48.376 --> 00:28:49.765
if you want at the very end.

00:28:49.766 --> 00:28:51.305
And I think as a society,

00:28:51.306 --> 00:28:54.210
we must develop policy to
address this challenge.

00:28:54.211 --> 00:28:56.690
So let's see what happened to

00:28:56.691 --> 00:28:59.290
the economy of
the last 40 years.

00:28:59.291 --> 00:29:01.560
So this is data that
was put together by

00:29:01.561 --> 00:29:04.015
Erik Brynjolfsson and
Andrew McAfee at MIT,

00:29:04.016 --> 00:29:06.075
and they have looked

00:29:06.076 --> 00:29:07.845
at four numbers that
are very important here.

00:29:07.846 --> 00:29:10.635
One is productivity, because
the economy grows is it

00:29:10.636 --> 00:29:12.120
because there are more people

00:29:12.121 --> 00:29:14.110
or people become
more productive?

00:29:14.111 --> 00:29:15.905
And so productivity growth is

00:29:15.906 --> 00:29:18.705
the engine of economic growth.

00:29:18.706 --> 00:29:21.285
And indeed you see here
that from the early 50's,

00:29:21.286 --> 00:29:24.540
for the next 30 years,

00:29:24.541 --> 00:29:28.445
productivity grows and with
productivity, GDP grows,

00:29:28.446 --> 00:29:32.725
the economy grows, jobs are
generated, and income grows.

00:29:32.726 --> 00:29:35.900
So this becomes
the Goldilock period.

00:29:35.901 --> 00:29:38.650
And you look at all these
numbers, and you think, "Okay.

00:29:38.651 --> 00:29:40.715
They're so nicely well aligned,

00:29:40.716 --> 00:29:43.075
you'd think there must
be some economic law that

00:29:43.076 --> 00:29:46.670
productivity growth drive
everything? Everything goes up.

00:29:46.671 --> 00:29:49.655
Except at what happened starting

00:29:49.656 --> 00:29:51.575
from roughly to mid 1980,

00:29:51.576 --> 00:29:54.465
we see a divergence.
We see a decoupling.

00:29:54.466 --> 00:29:57.285
And these cellular,
the productivity

00:29:57.286 --> 00:29:59.480
continued to grows
and the GDP grows,

00:29:59.481 --> 00:30:01.835
but we're not
creating enough jobs,

00:30:01.836 --> 00:30:04.535
and income has stagnated.

00:30:04.536 --> 00:30:09.180
And here's an example
of income stagnation.

00:30:09.181 --> 00:30:10.935
This is, I think,

00:30:10.936 --> 00:30:12.770
a nice way to look
at income stagnation.

00:30:12.771 --> 00:30:15.930
This is real median
yearly income

00:30:15.931 --> 00:30:19.025
of white men with
no college degree.

00:30:19.026 --> 00:30:21.645
And you can see that it's
actually has gone down

00:30:21.646 --> 00:30:25.050
between 1975 and 2015
has gone down.

00:30:25.051 --> 00:30:27.780
And this is, I have to
say, that few slides

00:30:27.781 --> 00:30:29.260
to me when I saw them, I gusp,

00:30:29.261 --> 00:30:31.905
because to realize that
here in the United States,

00:30:31.906 --> 00:30:34.400
for a very large sector
of the economy,

00:30:34.401 --> 00:30:37.995
thing have gone be now gone
for all now for 40, 50 years.

00:30:37.996 --> 00:30:39.695
It's shocking.

00:30:39.696 --> 00:30:44.025
And think about what we saw
in political rally in 2016.

00:30:44.026 --> 00:30:47.405
We saw angry white men.

00:30:47.406 --> 00:30:49.615
Now we used to see these men,

00:30:49.616 --> 00:30:51.610
usually there will be
somewhere in the middle east,

00:30:51.611 --> 00:30:53.330
and they usually will
say something like

00:30:53.331 --> 00:30:55.335
death to America,
death to America.

00:30:55.336 --> 00:30:57.195
And now, we are finding

00:30:57.196 --> 00:30:59.310
these people right
here in our midst.

00:30:59.311 --> 00:31:02.800
And you ask, "Okay, what
are they so angry about?"

00:31:02.801 --> 00:31:07.395
Well, this is what
they're angry about.

00:31:07.396 --> 00:31:09.715
Okay? They see the life quality

00:31:09.716 --> 00:31:12.430
declining for
a whole generation.

00:31:12.431 --> 00:31:15.015
And we also have been hearing

00:31:15.016 --> 00:31:17.875
a lot about growing inequality.

00:31:17.876 --> 00:31:19.955
So this shows you that
if you look again,

00:31:19.956 --> 00:31:24.215
this is between 1980 and 2013.

00:31:24.216 --> 00:31:26.850
And you see the bottom
90 percent decline.

00:31:26.851 --> 00:31:28.775
This is the top one percent.

00:31:28.776 --> 00:31:30.825
And then you see the people
who really did well are

00:31:30.826 --> 00:31:32.815
the top one percent of
the top one percent.

00:31:32.816 --> 00:31:35.710
They did really, really
well in this economy.

00:31:35.711 --> 00:31:40.225
And in particular what
it means is that part of

00:31:40.226 --> 00:31:44.215
the American dream was
of life getting better,

00:31:44.216 --> 00:31:45.695
except what you see here,

00:31:45.696 --> 00:31:50.295
is that the top one percent
used to own about 12 percent.

00:31:50.296 --> 00:31:52.150
We used to get about 12
percent of the income.

00:31:52.151 --> 00:31:53.865
It's going up to 20 percent.

00:31:53.866 --> 00:31:55.685
The bottom 50 percent
used to get

00:31:55.686 --> 00:31:59.235
about 20 percent of
the income. It has gone down.

00:31:59.236 --> 00:32:01.335
It's like some people
call it a tale of

00:32:01.336 --> 00:32:02.765
two countries where

00:32:02.766 --> 00:32:04.535
one country is doing
better and better,

00:32:04.536 --> 00:32:07.675
and one country is
doing worse and worse.

00:32:07.676 --> 00:32:10.075
And on top of it,

00:32:10.076 --> 00:32:11.475
we used to think that
this is a country

00:32:11.476 --> 00:32:13.655
that enable a lot
of social mobility.

00:32:13.656 --> 00:32:15.605
That is to say that
every generation,

00:32:15.606 --> 00:32:16.765
the next generation
will do better

00:32:16.766 --> 00:32:18.350
than the current generation.

00:32:18.351 --> 00:32:21.385
And what you see
here is that in 1940,

00:32:21.386 --> 00:32:23.005
if you're 30 years old,

00:32:23.006 --> 00:32:24.495
the poverty was 90 percent.

00:32:24.496 --> 00:32:26.175
If you're doing better than

00:32:26.176 --> 00:32:29.160
your parents when they
were 30 years old.

00:32:29.161 --> 00:32:30.545
Now it's 50-50.

00:32:30.546 --> 00:32:32.370
It's a contours where
they're going to do better

00:32:32.371 --> 00:32:38.115
or worse than your parents
when they were at similar age.

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:44.760
Okay. This is another
very significant thing.

00:32:44.761 --> 00:32:46.320
You'll hear a lot
about how unemployment

00:32:46.321 --> 00:32:48.360
now is very very low.

00:32:48.361 --> 00:32:51.220
Unemployment that is report in

00:32:51.221 --> 00:32:55.535
the news is a charade.
It's called U3.

00:32:55.536 --> 00:32:57.770
It's a concept invented by

00:32:57.771 --> 00:32:59.040
politicians to give us

00:32:59.041 --> 00:33:01.240
good news because
what does it measure?

00:33:01.241 --> 00:33:03.150
How many people that are looking

00:33:03.151 --> 00:33:05.485
for jobs are not finding a job?

00:33:05.486 --> 00:33:07.210
Does not measure
how many people stop

00:33:07.211 --> 00:33:09.065
looking for a job
because they gave up.

00:33:09.066 --> 00:33:11.895
And, this was to
me the second gasp.

00:33:11.896 --> 00:33:14.520
Labor vote
participate, these are

00:33:14.521 --> 00:33:17.030
people who are either
working or looking for a job.

00:33:17.031 --> 00:33:20.085
This are what's
called labor force.

00:33:20.086 --> 00:33:23.840
This is for men
between 25 and 54.

00:33:23.841 --> 00:33:25.630
The reason it's
important to isolate

00:33:25.631 --> 00:33:27.330
men for two reasons, why?

00:33:27.331 --> 00:33:28.420
The picture for women you can

00:33:28.421 --> 00:33:29.780
imagine is more complicated,

00:33:29.781 --> 00:33:32.685
they first enter the workforce
starting from the 60s,

00:33:32.686 --> 00:33:34.510
now it's also in decline but,

00:33:34.511 --> 00:33:36.055
it's a more nuanced picture.

00:33:36.056 --> 00:33:39.100
Also we're talk about
manufacturing, it's mostly men.

00:33:39.101 --> 00:33:43.890
Here you'll see there's been
going down now for 50 years,

00:33:43.891 --> 00:33:47.510
and it's even starker when you

00:33:47.511 --> 00:33:50.940
look what happened and you
include education level.

00:33:50.941 --> 00:33:52.380
You see the people
whose Bachelor Degree

00:33:52.381 --> 00:33:53.745
has gone down a little bit,

00:33:53.746 --> 00:33:55.340
but people with
high school degree or

00:33:55.341 --> 00:33:58.300
less is gone down even
more significantly.

00:33:58.301 --> 00:34:03.920
That means that, if you
look now at men with

00:34:03.921 --> 00:34:05.805
High School education or less

00:34:05.806 --> 00:34:09.875
probably about something like
one in four is not working.

00:34:09.876 --> 00:34:11.805
This is to me when
heard of it, I

00:34:11.806 --> 00:34:13.070
said, this is unbelievable.

00:34:13.071 --> 00:34:15.070
This is what we
think of a country in

00:34:15.071 --> 00:34:17.100
the midst of a depression,
when you hear about Greece,

00:34:17.101 --> 00:34:19.465
this unemployment level of

00:34:19.466 --> 00:34:24.045
25 percent not working
outside of the labor force.

00:34:24.046 --> 00:34:26.160
I cannot think of
anything to say,

00:34:26.161 --> 00:34:28.055
this is to me a National crisis

00:34:28.056 --> 00:34:30.855
except that we
don't hear about it.

00:34:30.856 --> 00:34:33.200
If you dig in Labor economy
will say, ''Oh Yeah,

00:34:33.201 --> 00:34:36.265
we know about this,'' but
this is not on the News.

00:34:36.266 --> 00:34:39.625
The 25 percent of working
class men are not working.

00:34:39.626 --> 00:34:43.220
Working class men
are not working.

00:34:44.680 --> 00:34:47.900
Somehow, I've develop of

00:34:47.901 --> 00:34:49.320
hiding and nothing
seem to work out,

00:34:49.321 --> 00:34:50.800
I apologize for that.

00:34:50.801 --> 00:34:52.840
And the final thing
that I want to show

00:34:52.841 --> 00:34:55.370
here on the label is for many,

00:34:55.371 --> 00:34:57.390
many years you saw that labor

00:34:57.391 --> 00:35:01.970
and capital are roughly
50-50 percent of the economy,

00:35:01.971 --> 00:35:04.960
and except again,
this was the case for

00:35:04.961 --> 00:35:07.100
many years and people thought
again it's an Economic Law.

00:35:07.101 --> 00:35:08.200
That economy have offered to be

00:35:08.201 --> 00:35:10.110
split between capital and labor,

00:35:10.111 --> 00:35:12.815
but we're seeing again
labor now declining

00:35:12.816 --> 00:35:16.590
for about 40 years
labor with declining.

00:35:16.591 --> 00:35:19.740
So, what's going on? I think

00:35:19.741 --> 00:35:23.875
the following plot kind of
explain what's going on.

00:35:23.876 --> 00:35:25.520
This was put by
David Autor who was

00:35:25.521 --> 00:35:27.575
a Labor Economist at MIT.

00:35:27.576 --> 00:35:29.400
And what he said, let's look and

00:35:29.401 --> 00:35:31.180
here it's a combination
of many plots.

00:35:31.181 --> 00:35:32.890
It's of smooth curve.

00:35:32.891 --> 00:35:36.380
It look at so-called
the Skill Spectrum.

00:35:36.381 --> 00:35:38.625
So, high-skilled jobs pay well,

00:35:38.626 --> 00:35:40.900
and low-skilled jobs
do not pay well.

00:35:40.901 --> 00:35:43.480
If you look at the skill level,

00:35:43.481 --> 00:35:45.910
are jobs being
generated or declining?

00:35:45.911 --> 00:35:47.870
And what you see is in

00:35:47.871 --> 00:35:51.175
the upper part of the skill
spectrum, we generate jobs.

00:35:51.176 --> 00:35:56.755
Okay? We know that here
in a Computer Science,

00:35:56.756 --> 00:35:58.945
the demand exceeds the supply.

00:35:58.946 --> 00:36:01.400
For example, this is
a high-skilled job.

00:36:01.401 --> 00:36:04.290
The very end of
the skill spectrum,

00:36:04.291 --> 00:36:05.910
we're also generating jobs.

00:36:05.911 --> 00:36:07.390
Why is that? Think of

00:36:07.391 --> 00:36:10.090
the job of cleaning table
of the busboy,

00:36:10.091 --> 00:36:11.985
cleaning table at
the restaurant.

00:36:11.986 --> 00:36:16.545
So, first of all, you want
a grand challenge in robotic?

00:36:16.546 --> 00:36:18.120
Do it, build a robot that

00:36:18.121 --> 00:36:19.630
can clean tables
in the restaurant.

00:36:19.631 --> 00:36:21.780
Okay, we don't have
the technology to do it.

00:36:21.781 --> 00:36:24.105
The level of dexterity,

00:36:24.106 --> 00:36:28.435
agility and situation
awareness that such a robot

00:36:28.436 --> 00:36:33.225
will have to exhibit is
way beyond what we can do.

00:36:33.226 --> 00:36:35.100
But we can take someone

00:36:35.101 --> 00:36:37.175
who has even not
a High School Degree,

00:36:37.176 --> 00:36:39.050
and pay the minimum wage

00:36:39.051 --> 00:36:40.135
and they are going
to do the job.

00:36:40.136 --> 00:36:41.570
So, there is
no reason to automate

00:36:41.571 --> 00:36:43.820
jobs at that part
of the spectrum.

00:36:43.821 --> 00:36:46.020
So, where do we automate jobs?

00:36:46.021 --> 00:36:48.575
In the middle of
the skill spectrum.

00:36:48.576 --> 00:36:50.660
Why are we automating jobs here?

00:36:50.661 --> 00:36:52.370
Take manufacturing, we pay

00:36:52.371 --> 00:36:57.120
manufacturing employee
$20 to $30 an hour.

00:36:57.121 --> 00:36:59.855
Well, it's a
significant investment.

00:36:59.856 --> 00:37:01.330
Okay? This is three times

00:37:01.331 --> 00:37:03.285
four times more
than minimum wage.

00:37:03.286 --> 00:37:05.090
What's the skill level?
It's medium.

00:37:05.091 --> 00:37:06.120
It's a fairly routine job.

00:37:06.121 --> 00:37:08.460
You keep doing the same
thing over and over.

00:37:08.461 --> 00:37:10.515
These are the things that
we know how to automate.

00:37:10.516 --> 00:37:13.395
So, here this is
the sweet spot for automation.

00:37:13.396 --> 00:37:15.930
What David Autor had shown,

00:37:15.931 --> 00:37:17.300
Autor also has show what happens

00:37:17.301 --> 00:37:18.970
over time and what you see is,

00:37:18.971 --> 00:37:20.790
this sweet spots get

00:37:20.791 --> 00:37:26.235
broader because as the
capability of robots increase,

00:37:26.236 --> 00:37:29.410
more and more jobs can
be defined as routine.

00:37:29.411 --> 00:37:32.705
It also moves to
the right, moves upwards.

00:37:32.706 --> 00:37:34.210
Okay? So, this is like

00:37:34.211 --> 00:37:36.655
the ozone hole, it
gets bigger and bigger.

00:37:36.656 --> 00:37:40.320
And, one way you
can look at it is by

00:37:40.321 --> 00:37:48.105
a looking at the income
according to Degree.

00:37:48.106 --> 00:37:51.320
And you can see that people
who have advanced Degrees,

00:37:51.321 --> 00:37:54.460
this is between 73 and now
the income has risen.

00:37:54.461 --> 00:37:56.380
This is true for also College,

00:37:56.381 --> 00:37:58.260
if you have just some College,

00:37:58.261 --> 00:37:59.460
if you didn't finish four year,

00:37:59.461 --> 00:38:03.570
you have some College,
you barely kept up,

00:38:03.571 --> 00:38:05.250
but if you have
High School or less you

00:38:05.251 --> 00:38:06.970
actually again you
have lost ground.

00:38:06.971 --> 00:38:09.510
These are the jobs
that there are lots of

00:38:09.511 --> 00:38:10.560
people who can do this job

00:38:10.561 --> 00:38:13.275
so, they're not
paid well at all.

