00:39:02.516 --> 00:39:04.815
It holds for Hispanics,
hold for Blacks.

00:39:04.816 --> 00:39:07.385
You can look abroad, it
happens in every country.

00:39:07.386 --> 00:39:09.305
Healthcare gets
better and better,

00:39:09.306 --> 00:39:11.745
but they found that White
in the United States,

00:39:11.746 --> 00:39:13.100
our mortality is going up.

00:39:13.101 --> 00:39:15.065
This is between 45 and 50 folks.

00:39:15.066 --> 00:39:16.590
These are middle-aged people,

00:39:16.591 --> 00:39:19.820
and again if you look
at it by Education,

00:39:19.821 --> 00:39:21.700
you find that this is related

00:39:21.701 --> 00:39:24.700
to lack of College Education.

00:39:24.701 --> 00:39:26.460
And if you dig
deeper and say okay,

00:39:26.461 --> 00:39:28.190
what are these people
dying from?

00:39:28.191 --> 00:39:31.355
You find the leading causes are

00:39:31.356 --> 00:39:35.880
suicide and drug overdose,

00:39:35.881 --> 00:39:38.040
we all hear now about
the opioid epidemics and

00:39:38.041 --> 00:39:40.030
liver diseases and all

00:39:40.031 --> 00:39:42.345
of these what they call
are disease of the despair.

00:39:42.346 --> 00:39:46.620
So, these people are
suffering from despair,

00:39:46.621 --> 00:39:49.100
and we can ask again
what's going on?

00:39:49.101 --> 00:39:50.580
I mean we're talking
about right now,

00:39:50.581 --> 00:39:51.855
for example I believe that

00:39:51.856 --> 00:39:55.910
opioid epidemics kill
more people than guns.

00:39:55.911 --> 00:39:57.370
Can you imagine what can kill

00:39:57.371 --> 00:39:59.960
more people than gun
in United States.

00:39:59.961 --> 00:40:08.405
And so, to top it this
is from March of 2016.

00:40:08.406 --> 00:40:11.955
This is before even Trump

00:40:11.956 --> 00:40:14.545
clinched the
Republican Nomination.

00:40:14.546 --> 00:40:17.540
The Washington Post,
look at the correlation

00:40:17.541 --> 00:40:21.255
between mortality
and voting for Trump,

00:40:21.256 --> 00:40:22.930
and they found out that

00:40:22.931 --> 00:40:25.980
the areas where there
was higher mortality,

00:40:25.981 --> 00:40:28.660
more despair, people
were voting for Trump.

00:40:28.661 --> 00:40:30.930
So, this economic development

00:40:30.931 --> 00:40:34.395
have serious political
consequences.

00:40:34.396 --> 00:40:39.400
This is what the economists
again put Counties by

00:40:39.401 --> 00:40:42.130
different index
of health metrics

00:40:42.131 --> 00:40:45.755
and you can see if you get
healthier it's becoming bluer,

00:40:45.756 --> 00:40:48.750
and as you get less
healthy it becomes red.

00:40:48.751 --> 00:40:51.230
Okay, so there is
a clear correlation here

00:40:51.231 --> 00:40:56.440
between Economics, Health
and Political inclinations.

00:40:56.441 --> 00:40:59.190
And to me this reminds
of very classic quote.

00:40:59.191 --> 00:41:02.210
I want to see if
anybody recognizes it.

00:41:02.211 --> 00:41:04.915
"All that is solid
melts into air,

00:41:04.916 --> 00:41:07.105
all that is holy is profaned,

00:41:07.106 --> 00:41:10.545
and man is at last compelled
to face with sober senses,

00:41:10.546 --> 00:41:12.185
his real conditions of life,

00:41:12.186 --> 00:41:15.265
and his relations
with his kind."

00:41:15.266 --> 00:41:21.800
Anybody recognizes it? Do we?

00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:26.390
I thought that you'll
have the proper,

00:41:26.391 --> 00:41:29.455
if anybody you should
had the proper education.

00:41:29.456 --> 00:41:34.465
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] it was
a good place to ignore all this.

00:41:34.466 --> 00:41:36.670
&gt;&gt; So there is

00:41:36.671 --> 00:41:39.220
a sense of dislocation
that reminds people,

00:41:39.221 --> 00:41:41.420
the feeling that
happened in the middle of

00:41:41.421 --> 00:41:43.910
the 19th century where and I'll

00:41:43.911 --> 00:41:47.060
come back in a few minutes
to the Industrial Revolution.

00:41:47.061 --> 00:41:49.645
Now, you look at all this data.

00:41:49.646 --> 00:41:52.440
And first of all, almost
any economic data,

00:41:52.441 --> 00:41:54.680
people question
because everything,

00:41:54.681 --> 00:41:57.940
for example, you look
at I said real income.

00:41:57.941 --> 00:42:00.550
Well real income, you
have to factor inflation.

00:42:00.551 --> 00:42:01.890
How do you measure inflation?

00:42:01.891 --> 00:42:03.740
There are different way
to measure inflation.

00:42:03.741 --> 00:42:06.835
Every piece of data here is
a somewhat controversial,

00:42:06.836 --> 00:42:09.900
but I think the general picture
gives something that even

00:42:09.901 --> 00:42:12.930
if you need pick it
every single plot here,

00:42:12.931 --> 00:42:15.245
you still see
Excel picture emerging.

00:42:15.246 --> 00:42:18.420
But as everybody tells you,

00:42:18.421 --> 00:42:21.720
correlation does
not imply causation.

00:42:21.721 --> 00:42:22.840
What is the cause of this?

00:42:22.841 --> 00:42:25.320
And economically, it's very
hard to determine causation.

00:42:25.321 --> 00:42:28.005
Okay? It's very, very
difficult to do it.

00:42:28.006 --> 00:42:32.280
So in 2014, an opinion poll
was conducted.

00:42:32.281 --> 00:42:33.790
The Pew Charitable
Foundation conducted

00:42:33.791 --> 00:42:36.235
a opinion poll amount
about 2,000 economists.

00:42:36.236 --> 00:42:40.600
And again, it's a poll so
it does not tell you truth.

00:42:40.601 --> 00:42:42.760
We have to take it
with a grain of salt.

00:42:42.761 --> 00:42:46.210
But there was a
significant really.

00:42:46.211 --> 00:42:48.100
It was a plurality for accepting

00:42:48.101 --> 00:42:50.585
the automation is
a central reason.

00:42:50.586 --> 00:42:53.030
Again, it's not the only reason
life is more complicated.

00:42:53.031 --> 00:42:57.280
It's a central reason
for wage stagnation.

00:42:57.281 --> 00:42:59.595
Now, I think
the picture has changed

00:42:59.596 --> 00:43:01.420
and partly because there
is a new study that just

00:43:01.421 --> 00:43:05.430
came up last year from
economists at MIT,

00:43:05.431 --> 00:43:08.200
and they went city
by city and look at

00:43:08.201 --> 00:43:12.160
deployments of an industrial
robots versus job losses.

00:43:12.161 --> 00:43:15.335
And again, remember,
this all correlation,

00:43:15.336 --> 00:43:16.900
but I think the weight of

00:43:16.901 --> 00:43:18.890
evidence is coming
more and more that

00:43:18.891 --> 00:43:21.140
automation plays a major role

00:43:21.141 --> 00:43:25.420
in the dislocation of
the working class people.

00:43:25.421 --> 00:43:28.550
Now, let's talk
about this argument.

00:43:28.551 --> 00:43:30.280
This time it is
different or not.

00:43:30.281 --> 00:43:32.375
So again, the argument
we hear a lot,

00:43:32.376 --> 00:43:35.265
technology destroying job
but it create new jobs.

00:43:35.266 --> 00:43:38.975
Jobs that we cannot even
fathom will be created.

00:43:38.976 --> 00:43:42.100
But let's do
a thought experiment.

00:43:42.101 --> 00:43:43.970
If you are working
AI, what is your goal?

00:43:43.971 --> 00:43:45.515
Your goal is to look

00:43:45.516 --> 00:43:49.000
one by one at
human capabilities and try

00:43:49.001 --> 00:43:52.230
to test this dream of
artificial intelligence

00:43:52.231 --> 00:43:53.960
to be able to build machine

00:43:53.961 --> 00:43:56.260
that will be just
as smart as people.

00:43:56.261 --> 00:43:58.090
Let's suppose that we succeed.

00:43:58.091 --> 00:44:00.315
I don't know if this will
take 20 years or 100 years.

00:44:00.316 --> 00:44:01.695
Let's suppose that we succeed.

00:44:01.696 --> 00:44:03.950
Somehow, because
this assumption means that there

00:44:03.951 --> 00:44:06.705
will always be something
that people are better at.

00:44:06.706 --> 00:44:08.960
And I don't see how you can

00:44:08.961 --> 00:44:11.025
argue that people will always
be better than machine.

00:44:11.026 --> 00:44:14.120
In fact, unless you go
into some metaphysics,

00:44:14.121 --> 00:44:17.265
we are some kind of
a biological machine.

00:44:17.266 --> 00:44:21.740
Evolution had millions of years
to perfect task.

00:44:21.741 --> 00:44:25.230
It may take some time for us
to compete with evolution,

00:44:25.231 --> 00:44:26.340
but I can't see

00:44:26.341 --> 00:44:28.110
anybody who come up
with an argument whereby

00:44:28.111 --> 00:44:29.440
all you people will always

00:44:29.441 --> 00:44:32.045
be better than
machine in some way.

00:44:32.046 --> 00:44:36.565
I just don't see
that. And in fact,

00:44:36.566 --> 00:44:38.925
if economists should
know about anything,

00:44:38.926 --> 00:44:42.085
it's about the concepts
as a tipping point.

00:44:42.086 --> 00:44:45.285
Okay. What is the tipping point?
To face transition.

00:44:45.286 --> 00:44:47.355
There was popular book
by Malcolm Gladwell,

00:44:47.356 --> 00:44:49.550
in 2005, demonstrated many,

00:44:49.551 --> 00:44:52.220
many cases where
a quantitative change

00:44:52.221 --> 00:44:54.630
finally lead to
a huge qualitative change.

00:44:54.631 --> 00:44:56.680
And in fact, what is

00:44:56.681 --> 00:44:59.350
the biggest qualitative
change that we

00:44:59.351 --> 00:45:02.600
have experienced in
human history? There are a few.

00:45:02.601 --> 00:45:04.495
You will say
Agricultural Revolution,

00:45:04.496 --> 00:45:06.780
then the Industrial Revolution.

00:45:06.781 --> 00:45:10.770
So imagine you are an economist
in the year 1700 and

00:45:10.771 --> 00:45:12.000
somebody asked you to predict

00:45:12.001 --> 00:45:14.765
economic growth for
the rest of the millennium.

00:45:14.766 --> 00:45:17.400
We'll say, "Well, I have

00:45:17.401 --> 00:45:19.820
economic data going back since

00:45:19.821 --> 00:45:22.475
Moses brought the two tablets,

00:45:22.476 --> 00:45:24.490
and these data show me that

00:45:24.491 --> 00:45:27.795
economic growth is always
maybe 0.1% per year."

00:45:27.796 --> 00:45:30.450
So we get confidence,
"I'm going to predict for

00:45:30.451 --> 00:45:32.050
the next 300 years
economic growth

00:45:32.051 --> 00:45:34.335
will be 0.1% per year."

00:45:34.336 --> 00:45:36.710
Well, you would be wrong

00:45:36.711 --> 00:45:39.295
because the Industrial
Revolution happened.

00:45:39.296 --> 00:45:42.080
And so are we now on

00:45:42.081 --> 00:45:45.090
the cusp of another kind

00:45:45.091 --> 00:45:46.920
of people talk about the
fourth Industrial Revolution?

00:45:46.921 --> 00:45:48.520
I don't know, but I don't know

00:45:48.521 --> 00:45:51.490
how we can exclude
that possibility.

00:45:51.491 --> 00:45:55.490
And in fact, this possibility,

00:45:55.491 --> 00:45:58.925
there was a very
parabola attributed

00:45:58.926 --> 00:46:01.060
appeared in a paper
by Wassily Leontief

00:46:01.061 --> 00:46:03.185
who was a Nobel Prize
winning economist.

00:46:03.186 --> 00:46:05.990
And he wrote about as
a parabola of two horses having

00:46:05.991 --> 00:46:10.620
a conversation in maybe 1910.

00:46:10.621 --> 00:46:13.885
In one horse is
a new Luddite horse.

00:46:13.886 --> 00:46:16.840
And he tell the other one,
other horse, a neoclassical,

00:46:16.841 --> 00:46:18.360
"Have you heard
about the Ford Model

00:46:18.361 --> 00:46:20.640
T. I'm incredibly worried.

00:46:20.641 --> 00:46:25.095
I think this new technology
will destroy jobs for horses.

00:46:25.096 --> 00:46:28.330
What will we do after
deployment of the automobile?"

00:46:28.331 --> 00:46:30.485
And the neoclassical horse says.

00:46:30.486 --> 00:46:32.860
"Come on. Technology
always destroy

00:46:32.861 --> 00:46:35.510
job for horses and it
create jobs for horses."

00:46:35.511 --> 00:46:37.560
There'll always be
new jobs for horses,

00:46:37.561 --> 00:46:40.465
job that we cannot yet
fathom and predict.

00:46:40.466 --> 00:46:42.810
Well, we know which horse was

00:46:42.811 --> 00:46:46.185
right because horse population
peaked in 1915,

00:46:46.186 --> 00:46:49.690
and today, there are no jobs
for horses. Horses are pets.

00:46:49.691 --> 00:46:53.035
Okay. This is the reality
if you're a horse.

00:46:53.036 --> 00:46:54.530
We can argue that maybe it's

00:46:54.531 --> 00:46:55.980
a better deal for
them. I don't know.

00:46:55.981 --> 00:47:01.310
That's a horse equine
philosophy debate.

00:47:01.311 --> 00:47:03.565
But in terms of jobs,

00:47:03.566 --> 00:47:06.330
there are no more jobs
for horses.

00:47:06.331 --> 00:47:09.790
And then people say, "Come on.

00:47:09.791 --> 00:47:11.185
The Industrial
Revolution happening.

00:47:11.186 --> 00:47:12.450
Look. Everything worked out.

00:47:12.451 --> 00:47:15.295
We're okay." And
my answer is, "Really?

00:47:15.296 --> 00:47:16.310
Have you learned your history?

00:47:16.311 --> 00:47:17.360
Of course, we are okay now.

00:47:17.361 --> 00:47:21.340
We're 200 years later
but first of all,

00:47:21.341 --> 00:47:23.935
to fully adapt to
Industrial Revolution,

00:47:23.936 --> 00:47:26.800
we had to invent a new thing,
a new institution.

00:47:26.801 --> 00:47:29.060
It's called the
social welfare state.

00:47:29.061 --> 00:47:30.710
It took 200 years.

00:47:30.711 --> 00:47:33.890
The maturity of
the social welfare state

00:47:33.891 --> 00:47:36.760
happens after World War II
in the '50s and in the '60s.

00:47:36.761 --> 00:47:39.550
Many, many things in
this country only happened in

00:47:39.551 --> 00:47:42.715
Great Society legislation of
Lyndon Johnson in the '60s.

00:47:42.716 --> 00:47:45.385
So it took us
200 years to adapt.

00:47:45.386 --> 00:47:48.070
Okay. It was not
an easy adaptation.

00:47:48.071 --> 00:47:49.730
There were two communist
revolution with

00:47:49.731 --> 00:47:52.070
about 100 million people
who died in them.

00:47:52.071 --> 00:47:54.440
Even in this country,
we forgot there

00:47:54.441 --> 00:47:56.690
were riots with
dozens of people,

00:47:56.691 --> 00:47:58.040
not black people, white people,

00:47:58.041 --> 00:47:59.445
being shot by police.

00:47:59.446 --> 00:48:02.110
We forgot how difficult

00:48:02.111 --> 00:48:04.290
was to adapt to
Industrial Revolution.

00:48:04.291 --> 00:48:06.325
And now, we have
learned nothing from it?

00:48:06.326 --> 00:48:08.100
We're going to repeat,
we are going to wait for

00:48:08.101 --> 00:48:10.165
another 200 years of pain and

00:48:10.166 --> 00:48:11.850
agony before we
learn to adapt to

00:48:11.851 --> 00:48:15.550
the next phase of technology?

00:48:15.551 --> 00:48:19.705
Of course, technology
creates up and destroy jobs.

00:48:19.706 --> 00:48:22.525
I mean this is obvious,

00:48:22.526 --> 00:48:24.280
but this is too simplistic.

00:48:24.281 --> 00:48:26.955
Will technology
create enough jobs?

00:48:26.956 --> 00:48:29.170
How fast will it create them?

00:48:29.171 --> 00:48:31.160
What about the skill level?

00:48:31.161 --> 00:48:34.790
So take a taxi driver
who lost his job

00:48:34.791 --> 00:48:36.580
because now the taxi
have been automated and

00:48:36.581 --> 00:48:38.460
it's only going to be the
first thing will be automated,

00:48:38.461 --> 00:48:40.300
will be long-distance
tracking will

00:48:40.301 --> 00:48:42.250
be the first thing to probably
to be automated before,

00:48:42.251 --> 00:48:44.755
we do it inside in
the center of cities.

00:48:44.756 --> 00:48:46.420
And you tell this truck driver

00:48:46.421 --> 00:48:48.105
who are very slowly
lost their job,

00:48:48.106 --> 00:48:49.735
"But we have openings,

00:48:49.736 --> 00:48:51.540
people who write software for

00:48:51.541 --> 00:48:55.990
autonomous trucks.
Please apply."

00:48:55.991 --> 00:49:00.380
Also, where are the jobs

00:49:00.381 --> 00:49:03.610
being now destroyed
in small cities,

00:49:03.611 --> 00:49:04.370
in the south, and in

00:49:04.371 --> 00:49:06.545
mid west while
they're being created?

00:49:06.546 --> 00:49:09.180
Lots of jobs in Seattle.

00:49:09.181 --> 00:49:11.120
Okay. Sell a job,

00:49:11.121 --> 00:49:12.780
sell a house in Kalamazoo,

00:49:12.781 --> 00:49:16.800
Michigan, and move to Seattle.
Easier said than done.

00:49:16.801 --> 00:49:19.780
People cannot move.
Location, location,

00:49:19.781 --> 00:49:23.130
location was said
about real estate.

00:49:23.131 --> 00:49:27.480
And this just came up
on about a month ago,

00:49:27.481 --> 00:49:28.955
LinkedIn top emerging jobs.

00:49:28.956 --> 00:49:30.625
Look at this list of jobs.

00:49:30.626 --> 00:49:33.255
Very exciting list of jobs.

00:49:33.256 --> 00:49:35.130
I don't even know what it is.

00:49:35.131 --> 00:49:39.125
What is it?

00:49:39.126 --> 00:49:41.010
The virtual reality,
okay. Thank you.

00:49:41.011 --> 00:49:42.720
Okay. How many of
these jobs goes to

00:49:42.721 --> 00:49:45.380
people with high school degree?

00:49:45.381 --> 00:49:48.590
So we have an economy
that creates

00:49:48.591 --> 00:49:52.230
fantastic jobs at the high end
of the skill ladder.

00:49:52.231 --> 00:49:55.390
&gt;&gt; It's really a bias
at recently [inaudible].

00:49:55.391 --> 00:49:57.080
&gt;&gt; Well, you look
at the other cases.

00:49:57.081 --> 00:49:58.085
But I will tell you.

00:49:58.086 --> 00:50:00.340
Let's come back to
other type of jobs.

00:50:00.341 --> 00:50:03.475
Okay so. This is very recently.

00:50:03.476 --> 00:50:06.640
Oren Etzioni, down
the street from here at

00:50:06.641 --> 00:50:10.575
an article in Wired magazine,

00:50:10.576 --> 00:50:11.960
"Workers displaced
by Automation Should

00:50:11.961 --> 00:50:14.865
Try a New Job: Caregiver."

00:50:14.866 --> 00:50:17.020
Well, okay.

00:50:17.021 --> 00:50:18.810
How much do we pay caregiver?

00:50:18.811 --> 00:50:20.230
Again, at the high end,

00:50:20.231 --> 00:50:22.395
if you're a physician
assistant, you'll be paid well.

00:50:22.396 --> 00:50:25.200
But it require
several years of training.

00:50:25.201 --> 00:50:29.255
If you're going to go for
a low-level caregiver,

00:50:29.256 --> 00:50:32.000
pays very little because
the skill level is low.

00:50:32.001 --> 00:50:34.505
The lots of people who can
do the job does not pay well.

00:50:34.506 --> 00:50:37.200
Generally, people move from

00:50:37.201 --> 00:50:41.280
manufacturing to services
lose 50% of their wages.

00:50:41.281 --> 00:50:42.855
This is a big, big loss.

00:50:42.856 --> 00:50:44.380
Any of you, consider losing

00:50:44.381 --> 00:50:47.215
50% of your income.
It's a big loss.

00:50:47.216 --> 00:50:49.690
But even beyond the money,

00:50:49.691 --> 00:50:52.885
take this big guy who used
to work on manufacturing,

00:50:52.886 --> 00:50:57.335
and we said, "We need to
go and wash old people."

00:50:57.336 --> 00:51:00.145
And many, you can say,
"Well, it's a noble job.

00:51:00.146 --> 00:51:02.520
My parent is dancer.

00:51:02.521 --> 00:51:04.115
These people will tell you

00:51:04.116 --> 00:51:06.680
very often, "That's
not who I am."

00:51:06.681 --> 00:51:09.860
And even if they were
willing to do that,

00:51:09.861 --> 00:51:12.075
if this big guy would show up

00:51:12.076 --> 00:51:13.840
in a nursing home and came here,

00:51:13.841 --> 00:51:16.020
he probably not get the job.

00:51:16.021 --> 00:51:17.730
They'll probably hire
a woman to do the job.

00:51:17.731 --> 00:51:19.490
So that cultural biases on

00:51:19.491 --> 00:51:22.770
all sides and we
consider irrational,

00:51:22.771 --> 00:51:24.225
we can complain about them,

00:51:24.226 --> 00:51:25.620
but people are not widgets.

00:51:25.621 --> 00:51:27.890
People are people. They
come with older biases.

00:51:27.891 --> 00:51:33.840
We cannot ignore it. And we
talk about quantity of jobs.

00:51:33.841 --> 00:51:35.850
So here is to me,
this is very start.

00:51:35.851 --> 00:51:38.110
Go back to Detroit just in 1990.

00:51:38.111 --> 00:51:41.280
And you find an industry
with a market value of

00:51:41.281 --> 00:51:43.010
about $65 billion in

00:51:43.011 --> 00:51:46.615
real dollars and more
than 1 million workers.

00:51:46.616 --> 00:51:49.435
Now go to Silicon
Valley of 2016.

00:51:49.436 --> 00:51:52.390
You find an industry, the
big three last company,

00:51:52.391 --> 00:51:56.790
which is a Google and
Apple and Facebook,

00:51:56.791 --> 00:52:00.530
and the market value then
was about $1.5 trillion,

00:52:00.531 --> 00:52:03.852
less than 200,000 employees.

00:52:03.853 --> 00:52:06.765
So, something about the number
of the new jobs very

00:52:06.766 --> 00:52:08.115
generic but the
numbers don't match

00:52:08.116 --> 00:52:10.850
the old job that we're losing.

00:52:10.851 --> 00:52:13.065
And it's amazing that we went

00:52:13.066 --> 00:52:16.440
through election
campaign in 2016,

00:52:16.441 --> 00:52:18.390
and we heard a lot about trade,

00:52:18.391 --> 00:52:20.360
we heard nothing
about automation.

00:52:20.361 --> 00:52:23.655
Not from Clinton,
and not from Trump.

00:52:23.656 --> 00:52:24.775
I'm not surprised
about Trump, but

00:52:24.776 --> 00:52:26.410
I'm a bit surprised
about Clinton.

00:52:26.411 --> 00:52:28.665
The only politician who
did talk about automation

00:52:28.666 --> 00:52:32.100
was President Obama who was
not running for anything,

00:52:32.101 --> 00:52:35.555
and he did observe that we are

00:52:35.556 --> 00:52:39.415
seeing jobs moving from
manufacturing to services,

00:52:39.416 --> 00:52:40.935
if we're moving and then,

00:52:40.936 --> 00:52:43.040
people are losing
half their income.

00:52:43.041 --> 00:52:44.835
And he said something
which I think is very

00:52:44.836 --> 00:52:47.160
important which is kind
of get to the bottom line.

00:52:47.161 --> 00:52:49.730
Everything we know about work

00:52:49.731 --> 00:52:52.440
in our life is
a fairly modern thing.

00:52:52.441 --> 00:52:54.455
It's not just
industrial evolution,

00:52:54.456 --> 00:52:57.795
industrial evolution plus
the social welfare state.

00:52:57.796 --> 00:52:59.515
Okay. And some people
call it the

00:52:59.516 --> 00:53:01.440
social compact that
we are living by.

00:53:01.441 --> 00:53:06.525
And it took 200 years to make
it happen. Right to strike.

00:53:06.526 --> 00:53:08.180
Remember, we saw the picture of

00:53:08.181 --> 00:53:10.135
people striking in Pittsburgh,

00:53:10.136 --> 00:53:12.060
and we saw the train
station burning,

00:53:12.061 --> 00:53:14.295
and more than 60
people shot by police.

00:53:14.296 --> 00:53:16.765
The right to unionize.
Abolish child labor.

00:53:16.766 --> 00:53:18.310
Working hour workweek.

00:53:18.311 --> 00:53:19.705
Equal pay for
women and minority.

00:53:19.706 --> 00:53:21.185
Health and safety laws. It took

00:53:21.186 --> 00:53:22.635
a long time for this to happen.

00:53:22.636 --> 00:53:24.115
And big fights over

00:53:24.116 --> 00:53:26.130
everything because always
there was resistance to

00:53:26.131 --> 00:53:30.280
every new improvement in
social and labor legislation.

00:53:30.281 --> 00:53:31.895
There's a new kid on

00:53:31.896 --> 00:53:34.625
the block called
universal basic income,

00:53:34.626 --> 00:53:36.345
and universal basic income,

00:53:36.346 --> 00:53:38.655
the idea is that we're
going to do away with

00:53:38.656 --> 00:53:41.585
a lot of welfare program
repressed by universal,

00:53:41.586 --> 00:53:45.565
every citizen gets a monthly
check from the government.

00:53:45.566 --> 00:53:48.925
It's radical idea, even
though it goes back,

00:53:48.926 --> 00:53:51.010
you can find Thomas Paine
writing about it.

00:53:51.011 --> 00:53:52.375
He said, because the land

00:53:52.376 --> 00:53:53.685
belonged to everyone originally,

00:53:53.686 --> 00:53:54.795
there should be,
everybody should get some

00:53:54.796 --> 00:53:56.300
kind of a lead dividend.

00:53:56.301 --> 00:53:58.935
You find, interesting
you'll find support

00:53:58.936 --> 00:54:01.740
for these ideas from the left
but also from the right.

00:54:01.741 --> 00:54:04.520
People like Milton Friedman
who was in favor,

00:54:04.521 --> 00:54:06.225
Charles Murray was in favor,

00:54:06.226 --> 00:54:07.585
and why do people,

00:54:07.586 --> 00:54:09.930
you can see why the socialist
would support it.

00:54:09.931 --> 00:54:11.925
Why would people on
the right support it?

00:54:11.926 --> 00:54:13.530
For two main reasons.

00:54:13.531 --> 00:54:16.220
One is the hate the nanny state,

00:54:16.221 --> 00:54:17.975
the hate the bureaucrats

00:54:17.976 --> 00:54:19.980
making decisions about
who should get money

00:54:19.981 --> 00:54:22.185
is a really simple to
get everybody money

00:54:22.186 --> 00:54:25.755
and kill huge parts
of the bureaucracy.

00:54:25.756 --> 00:54:28.095
And secondly, it will actually

00:54:28.096 --> 00:54:31.090
create a more
efficient labor force.

00:54:31.091 --> 00:54:32.595
And the reason is that

00:54:32.596 --> 00:54:33.645
a labor market will become

00:54:33.646 --> 00:54:34.705
more efficient
because right now,

00:54:34.706 --> 00:54:37.375
there are many people,
their job sucks.

00:54:37.376 --> 00:54:39.135
They're not paid
well. They cannot

00:54:39.136 --> 00:54:41.050
afford to look for another job.

00:54:41.051 --> 00:54:42.315
They have to be there.
They just don't have

00:54:42.316 --> 00:54:44.110
the flexibility to
look for another job.

00:54:44.111 --> 00:54:46.975
If the heaven is a safety
network, they would say,

00:54:46.976 --> 00:54:50.085
I hate this job, I'm going
to look for another job.

00:54:50.086 --> 00:54:52.585
So, these are the arguments
there are people

00:54:52.586 --> 00:54:55.830
on the left and on the right
that hate this idea.

00:54:55.831 --> 00:54:57.725
One of the reason that I'm

00:54:57.726 --> 00:54:59.145
going to be skeptical
about this idea

00:54:59.146 --> 00:55:00.195
personally is because it

00:55:00.196 --> 00:55:02.410
ignores the psychological
value of work.

00:55:02.411 --> 00:55:04.565
It only deal with
the socioeconomic value of work.

00:55:04.566 --> 00:55:07.135
It ignores the
psychological value

00:55:07.136 --> 00:55:09.515
of work where there
is a lot of research.

00:55:09.516 --> 00:55:11.100
At least, in today's culture,

00:55:11.101 --> 00:55:16.260
it plays such a huge role in
our own personal self-value.

00:55:16.261 --> 00:55:20.655
And I want to finish with
a quote quoting Jason Furman,

00:55:20.656 --> 00:55:22.105
who was the outgoing chair

00:55:22.106 --> 00:55:24.730
of Council of Economic Affairs
President Obama,

00:55:24.731 --> 00:55:27.475
and he says, "My worry is

00:55:27.476 --> 00:55:28.485
not that this time could

00:55:28.486 --> 00:55:30.135
be different when
it comes to AI,

00:55:30.136 --> 00:55:33.680
but at this time, could be
the same as what we have."

00:55:33.681 --> 00:55:36.290
That is if you look
at the past 50 years,

00:55:36.291 --> 00:55:39.205
and you just project
forward the same trend,

00:55:39.206 --> 00:55:40.285
you don't have to
change anything,

00:55:40.286 --> 00:55:40.835
you don't have to say,

00:55:40.836 --> 00:55:42.520
I'll be in another
phase transition.

00:55:42.521 --> 00:55:44.735
Just taken all these trend
we have seen,

00:55:44.736 --> 00:55:47.035
and just extend them
for the next 50 years,

00:55:47.036 --> 00:55:48.845
and we're in fairly bad shape.

00:55:48.846 --> 00:55:50.855
So, I think we're facing, to me,

00:55:50.856 --> 00:55:52.400
that if you ask me that kind of

00:55:52.401 --> 00:55:54.860
too huge policy challenges

00:55:54.861 --> 00:55:57.125
and domestic policy challenges,

00:55:57.126 --> 00:55:59.150
one is climate change,

00:55:59.151 --> 00:56:02.470
and the other one is what I
would call shared prosperity.

00:56:02.471 --> 00:56:03.895
I mean, there's no question
that technology will

00:56:03.896 --> 00:56:05.805
create tremendous
amount of wealth.

00:56:05.806 --> 00:56:07.625
How do we do in such a way

00:56:07.626 --> 00:56:09.625
that the country as
a whole benefits from it?

00:56:09.626 --> 00:56:11.355
Thank you very much. Ben.

00:56:11.356 --> 00:56:23.940
&gt;&gt; Sir, I'm going
back to the horses.

00:56:23.941 --> 00:56:26.550
The population of the earth

00:56:26.551 --> 00:56:28.565
might decrease over time, right?

00:56:28.566 --> 00:56:30.745
And so, one possible solution to

00:56:30.746 --> 00:56:34.255
this conundrum is that the
human population just shrinks.

00:56:34.256 --> 00:56:37.215
Do you think that's
a reasonable possibility?

00:56:37.216 --> 00:56:39.435
And if so, could it happen
fast enough that it would

00:56:39.436 --> 00:56:40.460
actually avoid some of

00:56:40.461 --> 00:56:41.945
the challenges
you're talking about?

00:56:41.946 --> 00:56:44.090
&gt;&gt; So, let me
broaden your issue,

00:56:44.091 --> 00:56:46.590
and it said that
this talk ignore one huge,

00:56:46.591 --> 00:56:49.395
another huge trend
and demographics.

00:56:49.396 --> 00:56:51.215
And demographics is currently

00:56:51.216 --> 00:56:53.650
one thing we see is
aging of the population,

00:56:53.651 --> 00:56:55.745
and so what the book says, Oh,

00:56:55.746 --> 00:56:56.865
we need all the robots
we can have

00:56:56.866 --> 00:56:58.465
to take care of
the older people.

00:56:58.466 --> 00:57:00.955
Okay. This is one,
and the other one

00:57:00.956 --> 00:57:03.710
is that as societies
get richer generally,

00:57:03.711 --> 00:57:06.110
the reproductive rate declines.

00:57:06.111 --> 00:57:08.335
So, demographer kind
of thing that

00:57:08.336 --> 00:57:10.395
we will peak at
about 10 billion,

00:57:10.396 --> 00:57:11.970
then we'll start declining.

00:57:11.971 --> 00:57:13.925
I have to say getting to 10

00:57:13.926 --> 00:57:15.695
billion they're talking
about somehow the

00:57:15.696 --> 00:57:18.175
next like 2100 that will

00:57:18.176 --> 00:57:20.695
mean just too far to
really think what happened.

00:57:20.696 --> 00:57:22.070
I mean, I'm just worried about

00:57:22.071 --> 00:57:23.945
how do we navigate the next 25,

00:57:23.946 --> 00:57:25.575
30 years of 2100,

00:57:25.576 --> 00:57:27.960
is it just a bit too far for me?

00:57:27.961 --> 00:57:30.130
How aging interact with it?

00:57:30.131 --> 00:57:31.905
This is a huge question,
and I have to say,

00:57:31.906 --> 00:57:34.170
I have not seen a really,
really good answers about it.

00:57:34.171 --> 00:57:37.440
So, it's a very good questions,
what's going to be?

00:57:37.441 --> 00:57:39.645
How does technology and

00:57:39.646 --> 00:57:42.615
automation interact with
aging of their workforce?

00:57:42.616 --> 00:57:44.985
I have not seen very,
very good answers to that.

00:57:44.986 --> 00:57:47.835
I think nobody knows, yeah,
but it's a good question.

00:57:47.836 --> 00:57:50.900
Yes? Speak up so I can hear you.

00:57:50.901 --> 00:57:52.825
&gt;&gt; Yes, so there's
sort of an observation,

00:57:52.826 --> 00:57:54.535
so you sort of handled 2020

00:57:54.536 --> 00:57:56.525
of the idea of
universal basic income.

00:57:56.526 --> 00:57:58.565
But the thing that you
objected to about it

00:57:58.566 --> 00:58:02.620
was that there is the
psychological value of doing work.

00:58:02.621 --> 00:58:06.135
So, I wonder how well
studied isn't like.

00:58:06.136 --> 00:58:08.100
Suppose we doubled minimum wage.

00:58:08.101 --> 00:58:09.995
Unemployment is very
little right now.

00:58:09.996 --> 00:58:11.920
Undoubtedly, it would rise some,

00:58:11.921 --> 00:58:16.100
but for other people
who have remaining work,

00:58:16.101 --> 00:58:17.825
they would be getting
substantially more than it

00:58:17.826 --> 00:58:19.585
might be a way of forcing

00:58:19.586 --> 00:58:21.235
the people that are
making a lot more money

00:58:21.236 --> 00:58:22.305
right now to value

00:58:22.306 --> 00:58:26.120
the services that those people
are providing more highly,

00:58:26.121 --> 00:58:28.605
and maybe a way of
spreading things out.

00:58:28.606 --> 00:58:31.855
That might be
another way other than

00:58:31.856 --> 00:58:33.485
universal basic
income that still

00:58:33.486 --> 00:58:37.230
preserves that
the standard that you like.

00:58:37.231 --> 00:58:41.395
&gt;&gt; There are a lot of speculation,
what happened, I mean,

00:58:41.396 --> 00:58:43.345
look part of the issue
is, what happened when

00:58:43.346 --> 00:58:44.825
right now economy is based

00:58:44.826 --> 00:58:46.185
on people working and consuming.

00:58:46.186 --> 00:58:47.965
What happen if more and
more people aren't working?

00:58:47.966 --> 00:58:50.125
What will be the engine
of economic growth?

00:58:50.126 --> 00:58:51.765
And people who says, well,

00:58:51.766 --> 00:58:54.235
in a world where

00:58:54.236 --> 00:58:55.815
most of the things are
produced by robots,

00:58:55.816 --> 00:58:57.910
it will be suddenly
a new human economy.

00:58:57.911 --> 00:59:00.385
Just like that people are
now willing now to pay

00:59:00.386 --> 00:59:02.485
more for handmade things.

00:59:02.486 --> 00:59:03.815
Somehow, there would
be a flowers of

00:59:03.816 --> 00:59:05.760
all human economy
that were created.

00:59:05.761 --> 00:59:07.855
My answer is, nobody knows.

00:59:07.856 --> 00:59:09.765
This is the future.
Nobody could have

00:59:09.766 --> 00:59:13.425
predicted 100 years ago
how society will evolve.

00:59:13.426 --> 00:59:15.675
I think we have
a polymer hence now,

00:59:15.676 --> 00:59:17.230
and I don't know that
we can come up with

00:59:17.231 --> 00:59:20.335
the ultimate answer but I
think on the other hand,

00:59:20.336 --> 00:59:23.245
ignoring, I mean, telling
people, don't worry.

00:59:23.246 --> 00:59:26.185
If you tell people,
some people will say,

00:59:26.186 --> 00:59:28.575
look, it's hard for you but
your children will do okay.

00:59:28.576 --> 00:59:29.945
Maybe people can take it.

00:59:29.946 --> 00:59:32.145
But if you tell people,
going to be hard for you,

00:59:32.146 --> 00:59:33.835
going to be hard
for your children,

00:59:33.836 --> 00:59:35.840
maybe your grandchildren
will be okay.

00:59:35.841 --> 00:59:37.835
For most people, it's
not an acceptable answer.

00:59:37.836 --> 00:59:38.855
Right? They would like to see.

00:59:38.856 --> 00:59:39.895
So, we need to come up do

00:59:39.896 --> 00:59:41.670
something without
worrying so much,

00:59:41.671 --> 00:59:43.035
what happened in
the very long term?

00:59:43.036 --> 00:59:43.600
And my answer is,

00:59:43.601 --> 00:59:45.190
nobody knows what will
happen in a very long-term.

00:59:45.191 --> 00:59:47.640
There was a question
there in the back?

00:59:47.641 --> 00:59:50.705
&gt;&gt; You just answered, thank you.

00:59:50.706 --> 00:59:52.905
&gt;&gt; So, I have a question

00:59:52.906 --> 00:59:54.545
regarding the importance of

00:59:54.546 --> 00:59:56.020
the economy in the first place.

00:59:56.021 --> 00:59:59.675
Some anthropologists argue
that civilization is not

00:59:59.676 --> 01:00:01.285
a natural state for human

01:00:01.286 --> 01:00:03.690
that actually
hunters and gathers,

01:00:03.691 --> 01:00:05.455
that's what we want to be.

01:00:05.456 --> 01:00:08.675
